WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.706
Safe file handling includes availability.

2
00:00:02.786 --> 00:00:06.959
A file operation can be correct from an
authorization perspective and still unsafe

3
00:00:07.039 --> 00:00:09.960
if it exhausts memory, fills disk, ties up
worker threads,

4
00:00:10.040 --> 00:00:14.213
leaks file descriptors, overwhelms a
scanner, or saturates a downstream service.

5
00:00:14.293 --> 00:00:17.811
File operations consume memory, CPU, disk,
bandwidth, file handles,

6
00:00:17.891 --> 00:00:21.051
thread pools, queues, parser capacity, and
storage capacity.

7
00:00:21.131 --> 00:00:23.098
Those resources need explicit limits.

8
00:00:23.178 --> 00:00:27.172
Avoid loading large or untrusted files
fully into memory unless the use case and

9
00:00:27.252 --> 00:00:28.206
limits justify it.

10
00:00:28.286 --> 00:00:32.281
Use streaming approaches where
appropriate, with bounded buffers, timeouts,

11
00:00:32.361 --> 00:00:33.374
and error handling.

12
00:00:33.454 --> 00:00:36.673
Apply file size limits, request body
limits, file count limits,

13
00:00:36.753 --> 00:00:40.867
timeout controls, concurrency limits,
storage quotas, and queue backpressure.

14
00:00:40.947 --> 00:00:45.001
The right limit depends on the workflow,
but unlimited should not be the default.

15
00:00:45.081 --> 00:00:48.002
Handle partial reads, partial writes,
aborted requests,

16
00:00:48.082 --> 00:00:51.182
failed transfers, interrupted threads, and
retry behavior.

17
00:00:51.262 --> 00:00:55.018
Close streams and file handles reliably
using appropriate Java patterns.

18
00:00:55.098 --> 00:00:59.033
A file that fails halfway through
generation, upload, download, conversion,

19
00:00:59.113 --> 00:01:02.869
or transfer should not leave a confusing
artifact that later code treats as

20
00:01:02.949 --> 00:01:03.485
complete.

21
00:01:03.565 --> 00:01:07.381
State and cleanup should reflect whether
the operation succeeded, failed,

22
00:01:07.461 --> 00:01:09.190
was canceled, or is still pending.

23
00:01:09.270 --> 00:01:12.489
Cleanup is part of the feature, not
janitorial work to add later.

24
00:01:12.569 --> 00:01:16.265
Remove abandoned temporary files, failed
exports, stale cache entries,

25
00:01:16.345 --> 00:01:19.624
expired generated files, and incomplete
processing artifacts.

26
00:01:19.704 --> 00:01:23.877
Monitor storage growth, processing
latency, parser failures, scanner failures,

27
00:01:23.957 --> 00:01:25.627
queue age, and cleanup failures.

28
00:01:25.707 --> 00:01:28.509
A healthy file system is one where
expected files exist,

29
00:01:28.589 --> 00:01:30.437
unexpected files are investigated,

30
00:01:30.517 --> 00:01:33.140
and old temporary data does not quietly
accumulate.
